// std/log.jtr — structured logging: one record, written once, rendered two ways.
//
// TIER: `std`. It reaches the operating system through exactly two things it is GIVEN — a
// `time.Clock` for the timestamp and a `writer.Writer` for the destination — so a `Logger`
// over `time.manual()` and `writer.to_buffer()` touches nothing at all and runs in a test.
// That is the same discipline `std/runtime` follows, and for the same payoff.
//
// ─── The rule this module exists to obey ──────────────────────────────────────
//
// **No hidden logger.** There is no `log.info(…)` free function, no ambient default, and no
// initialization that happens on first use. You construct a `Logger`, you hand it to whoever
// logs, and its destination and clock are visible in the signature that made it. A global
// logger is a hidden global with an open file handle and a clock attached, and this tree has
// spent a lot of effort not having hidden globals.
//
// ─── What "structured" buys, and the one property it rests on ─────────────────
//
// A record is `(timestamp, level, message, fields…)`, and **it is always exactly one line**.
// Every value is escaped, so a message containing a quote, a newline or an `=` cannot end
// the record early, split it in two, or invent a field. That is the whole difference between
// a log a program can read and a log a program can only nearly read — and it is also the
// property hand-rolled loggers break first, which is why `log_demo.jtr` PARSES ITS OWN
// OUTPUT back and asserts what it finds.
//
// **The escaping is `std/json`'s, deliberately, in both formats.** A quoted logfmt value is
// a JSON string, produced by the same `json.put_str` that the JSON renderer uses and that
// `json_test` already covers. Two escape tables would be two places to be wrong about the
// same question, and the second one would be the one nobody tested.
//
// ─── Two formats, one record-building API ─────────────────────────────────────
//
//   LOG_LOGFMT   ts=1000 level=info msg="watch fired" path="a b.txt" count=3
//   LOG_JSON     {"ts":1000,"level":"info","msg":"watch fired","path":"a b.txt","count":3}
//
// The format is a property of the `Logger`, chosen once at `make`, and the calls that build
// a record are identical either way. That is not a convenience: it means the decision of
// what to record is made in the code that knows, and the decision of how to ship it is made
// where the log is consumed — and neither one can be changed by editing the other.
// `the_same_record_renders_in_both_formats` builds one record twice and asserts both.
//
// ─── Filtering happens BEFORE formatting, and forgetting to check is SAFE ─────
//
// `begin` returns false when the record's level is below the logger's minimum, so the hot
// path is
//
//     if log.begin(lg, LOG_DEBUG, "cache probe") {
//         log.kv_str(lg, "key", k)
//         log.end(lg, s, buf)
//     }
//
// and the cost of a filtered DEBUG record is one integer comparison. **But a caller who
// omits the `if` is CORRECT, not merely lucky**: `begin` marks the record closed, every
// `kv_*` on a closed record is a no-op, and `end` reports false. Filtering is an
// optimisation the caller may take, never a rule the caller must obey — a logger where
// forgetting the guard corrupts the next record is a logger that will corrupt the next
// record.
//
// ─── Every way a record can fail to appear is COUNTED, and they are distinct ──
//
//   dropped     below the minimum level        → lower the level
//   truncated   too big for the record buffer  → make the buffer bigger
//   abandoned   `begin` without a matching `end` → fix the missing `end`
//
// Three causes, three counters, because the three FIXES are different and a single "lost"
// number would tell a caller nothing about which to apply. This is `std/sink`'s lesson —
// `sink.overflowed` exists because a truncated write that reports success is how
// `writer_demo` once produced `user=alice balan` while every oracle answered truthfully.
//
// **A truncated record is NOT emitted.** A half-written JSON object is unparseable and a
// half-written logfmt line silently loses its last fields, so shipping one would put
// corruption into the stream that a reader cannot detect. Instead the record is dropped and
// — when the buffer can hold it — a minimal, VALID record announcing the loss takes its
// place, so the log reports its own gap in band. That is the same choice `std/syswatch`
// makes with `WATCH_OVERFLOW`: loss that a consumer can see beats loss it cannot.
//
// ─── What this does NOT do ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// No log rotation, no sampling, no rate limiting, no async/buffered shipping, no context
// inheritance (a "child logger with these fields pre-set"). Each is a policy, and each wants
// a decision this module has no basis to make for every caller. Rotation in particular
// belongs with the append-only log (the brief's §1.10), not here.
//
// Worked example:  examples/std/log_demo.jtr
// Run the tests:   jestyrc test examples/std/log_test.jtr

import "sink"
import "json"
import "writer"
import "time"

// ---- levels -------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Five, ordered, with a sixth that admits nothing. Ordering is the whole point — a level is
// compared against the logger's minimum, so the numbers are the API and not an enum's
// private business. (An `enum` would be the more Jestyr-ish spelling and is deliberately not
// used: enums have no `Eq` and no ordering, so every comparison would be a `match`.)

pub const LOG_TRACE: i32 = 0
pub const LOG_DEBUG: i32 = 1
pub const LOG_INFO: i32 = 2
pub const LOG_WARN: i32 = 3
pub const LOG_ERROR: i32 = 4
// A minimum that admits nothing. Not a level a record can BE — `begin(lg, LOG_OFF, …)` is a
// caller error the same way `begin(lg, 99, …)` is — which is why it is spelled far above the
// real ones instead of as `LOG_ERROR + 1`.
pub const LOG_OFF: i32 = 99

@no_alloc @no_os pub fn level_name(lv: i32) -> str {
    if lv <= LOG_TRACE { return "trace" }
    if lv == LOG_DEBUG { return "debug" }
    if lv == LOG_INFO { return "info" }
    if lv == LOG_WARN { return "warn" }
    if lv == LOG_ERROR { return "error" }
    return "off"
}

// ---- formats ------------------------------------------------------------------

pub const LOG_LOGFMT: i32 = 0
pub const LOG_JSON: i32 = 1

// ---- the handle ---------------------------------------------------------------

// **The logger owns the buffer a record is built in**, which is the one place this module
// departs from the tree's usual "storage stays with the caller" rule, and the reason is that
// the caller has no business sizing it per call: a record's size is a property of the logger
// (its format and its fields), not of the call site. Owning it also makes truncation the
// LOGGER's business, which is what lets it be counted and announced instead of silently
// landing in someone else's sink.
//
// The DESTINATION's sink still travels as a parameter, and only on `end` — one call rather
// than all of them. That is `std/writer`'s own convention: the sink and its buffer travel on
// every write and are simply unused for a stream target, so a routine that logs is
// polymorphic over where the log goes.
pub struct Logger {
    clock: time.Clock,
    w: writer.Writer,
    jw: json.Writer,
    rec: Sink,
    raw: *mut u8,
    cap: usize,
    min: i32,
    fmt: i32,
    open: bool,
    emitted: i64,
    dropped: i64,
    truncated: i64,
    abandoned: i64,
}

// The record buffer's default size. Generous for a line of key/value pairs and small enough
// that a program holding many loggers does not notice; `make_sized` is there for the caller
// who knows better.
pub const LOG_RECORD_CAP: usize = 4096

// `take` for both handles, because the logger STORES them: a Jestyr borrow is second-class
// and may not outlive the call, so a logger that borrowed its clock could not outlive the
// frame that made it. The same signature `runtime.make` carries, for the same reason.
pub fn make(take c: time.Clock, take w: writer.Writer, min: i32, fmt: i32) -> Logger {
    return make_sized(c, w, min, fmt, LOG_RECORD_CAP)
}

pub fn make_sized(take c: time.Clock, take w: writer.Writer, min: i32, fmt: i32, cap: usize) -> Logger {
    var raw: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, cap)
    return Logger{ clock: c, w: w, jw: json.writer(), rec: sink.new(), raw: raw, cap: cap,
                   min: min, fmt: fmt, open: false, emitted: 0, dropped: 0, truncated: 0,
                   abandoned: 0 }
}

// Release the record buffer. The clock and the writer own nothing, so there is nothing else
// to give back.
pub fn free(mut lg: Logger) {
    if lg.raw != null {
        free_ptr(lg.raw)
        lg.raw = null
    }
    lg.open = false
}

@no_alloc @no_os pub fn min_level(read lg: Logger) -> i32 { return lg.min }
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn format(read lg: Logger) -> i32 { return lg.fmt }

// Change the threshold at runtime — the one knob an operator actually turns.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn set_min_level(mut lg: Logger, min: i32) { lg.min = min }

// Would a record at `lv` be emitted? For the caller who wants to skip building the ARGUMENTS
// as well as the record — `enabled` answers before anything is evaluated, where `begin`
// answers after the message string exists.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn enabled(read lg: Logger, lv: i32) -> bool { return lv >= lg.min }

// Records the CALLER built that reached the destination.
//
// A truncation notice is deliberately NOT counted here: it is this module's record, not the
// caller's, and folding it in would make `emitted` answer a question nobody asked ("lines in
// the stream") instead of the one they did ("records of mine that got through"). `truncated`
// is where the notice's cause is counted.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn emitted(read lg: Logger) -> i64 { return lg.emitted }
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn dropped(read lg: Logger) -> i64 { return lg.dropped }
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn truncated(read lg: Logger) -> i64 { return lg.truncated }
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn abandoned(read lg: Logger) -> i64 { return lg.abandoned }

// Is a record currently being built? A caller should not need this; a test does, and so does
// anyone debugging a missing `end`.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn building(read lg: Logger) -> bool { return lg.open }

// The logger OWNS its clock (`take` at construction), so time is driven through the logger
// and not through the caller's copy — the same signature and the same reason as
// `runtime.advance`. A test that kept advancing its own `Clock` would be advancing one
// nothing reads, and every timestamp would come out identical.
pub fn advance(mut lg: Logger, by: i64) -> bool {
    return time.advance(lg.clock, by)
}

// What the next record's `ts` will be.
pub fn now(mut lg: Logger) -> i64 {
    return time.read_nanos(lg.clock)
}

// ---- building a record --------------------------------------------------------

// The logger's own buffer, as a slice. Rebuilt per call rather than stored, because a struct
// field cannot hold a borrow.
@no_alloc @no_os fn rbuf(read lg: Logger) -> []u8 {
    return slice(u8, lg.raw, lg.cap)
}

// Start a record. Returns whether it will be emitted.
//
// A `begin` while another record is open ABANDONS that record and counts it. That is the
// shape of the realistic bug — an early `return` between `begin` and `end` — and leaving it
// uncounted would make the missing record indistinguishable from one that was never written.
pub fn begin(mut lg: Logger, lv: i32, read msg: str) -> bool {
    // **A freed logger refuses rather than writing through a null buffer.** `free` is the
    // only way a `Logger` becomes unusable, and logging after it is an ordinary
    // shutdown-ordering bug — the kind that would otherwise crash somewhere far from the
    // `free`. It is not counted as `dropped`: nothing was filtered, the logger is simply
    // gone. Every other entry point is already covered, because `free` clears `open` and a
    // closed record makes `kv_*` and `end` no-ops.
    if lg.raw == null { return false }
    if lg.open { lg.abandoned = lg.abandoned + 1 }
    lg.open = false
    if lv < lg.min {
        lg.dropped = lg.dropped + 1
        return false
    }
    let now: i64 = time.read_nanos(lg.clock)
    var buf: []u8 = rbuf(lg)
    sink.reset(lg.rec)
    lg.jw = json.writer()
    lg.open = true

    if lg.fmt == LOG_JSON {
        json.begin_object(lg.jw, lg.rec, buf)
        json.key(lg.jw, lg.rec, buf, "ts")
        json.put_i64(lg.jw, lg.rec, buf, now)
        json.key(lg.jw, lg.rec, buf, "level")
        json.put_str(lg.jw, lg.rec, buf, level_name(lv))
        json.key(lg.jw, lg.rec, buf, "msg")
        json.put_str(lg.jw, lg.rec, buf, msg)
        return true
    }
    sink.put_str(lg.rec, buf, "ts=")
    sink.put_i64(lg.rec, buf, now)
    sink.put_str(lg.rec, buf, " level=")
    sink.put_str(lg.rec, buf, level_name(lv))
    sink.put_str(lg.rec, buf, " msg=")
    put_logfmt_value(lg.rec, buf, msg)
    return true
}

// **There are no per-level openers (`log.info(lg, m)`), and the reason is a keyword.**
//
// `error` is reserved by the grammar, so the set cannot be spelled: `trace`/`debug`/`info`/
// `warn` parse and `error` does not. Every way out is worse than having none. An asymmetric
// four-plus-`log_error` puts the odd spelling on the level a reader most needs to get right;
// a uniformly prefixed `begin_info`/`begin_error` set is `begin(lg, LOG_INFO, …)` with five
// more names and no more capability; and `std/diag`'s dodge (`sev_error`, `new_error`) works
// for an enum variant precisely because nobody types it forty times a file.
//
// So `begin` is the only opener, and it is better than it looks: the level constant sits at
// the call site where it greps, and it is the SAME vocabulary as `enabled(lg, LOG_DEBUG)`
// and `set_min_level(lg, LOG_WARN)` rather than a second one that has to be kept in step.
//
// (`read` is unspellable for the same reason, one module over — see `std/syswatch`'s header
// on `read(2)`. The pattern is worth naming: the obvious API name is sometimes already the
// grammar's, and the fix is a better API rather than a worse spelling.)

// A string field. A no-op on a closed record — see the header on why forgetting the guard is
// safe rather than merely tolerated.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn kv_str(mut lg: Logger, read k: str, read v: str) {
    if lg.open == false { return }
    var buf: []u8 = rbuf(lg)
    if lg.fmt == LOG_JSON {
        json.key(lg.jw, lg.rec, buf, k)
        json.put_str(lg.jw, lg.rec, buf, v)
        return
    }
    sink.put(lg.rec, buf, 32 as u8)
    sink.put_str(lg.rec, buf, k)
    sink.put(lg.rec, buf, 61 as u8)          // '='
    put_logfmt_value(lg.rec, buf, v)
}

@no_alloc @no_os pub fn kv_i64(mut lg: Logger, read k: str, v: i64) {
    if lg.open == false { return }
    var buf: []u8 = rbuf(lg)
    if lg.fmt == LOG_JSON {
        json.key(lg.jw, lg.rec, buf, k)
        json.put_i64(lg.jw, lg.rec, buf, v)
        return
    }
    sink.put(lg.rec, buf, 32 as u8)
    sink.put_str(lg.rec, buf, k)
    sink.put(lg.rec, buf, 61 as u8)
    sink.put_i64(lg.rec, buf, v)
}

@no_alloc @no_os pub fn kv_bool(mut lg: Logger, read k: str, v: bool) {
    if lg.open == false { return }
    var buf: []u8 = rbuf(lg)
    if lg.fmt == LOG_JSON {
        json.key(lg.jw, lg.rec, buf, k)
        json.put_bool(lg.jw, lg.rec, buf, v)
        return
    }
    sink.put(lg.rec, buf, 32 as u8)
    sink.put_str(lg.rec, buf, k)
    sink.put(lg.rec, buf, 61 as u8)
    sink.put_bool(lg.rec, buf, v)
}

// Finish the record and ship it. Returns whether it reached the destination.
//
// `s`/`buf` are the DESTINATION's sink, used only by a buffer target and simply ignored by
// stdout and stderr — `std/writer`'s convention, which is what keeps a logging routine
// polymorphic over where its output goes.
//
// One `writer.line` per record, never one per field: the stream targets terminate every
// write with a newline of their own, so a record assembled piecewise would arrive as one
// line per piece. That is exactly the case `std/writer`'s header says to build in a `Sink`
// and write once, and a log record is the archetype of it.
pub fn end(mut lg: Logger, mut s: Sink, mut buf: []u8) -> bool {
    if lg.open == false { return false }
    lg.open = false
    var rb: []u8 = rbuf(lg)
    if lg.fmt == LOG_JSON { json.end_object(lg.jw, lg.rec, rb) }

    // **A truncated record is not emitted.** See the header: an unparseable line in the
    // stream is worse than a missing one, because a reader cannot tell it happened.
    if sink.overflowed(lg.rec) {
        lg.truncated = lg.truncated + 1
        return announce_truncation(lg, s, buf)
    }
    let text: str = sink.view(lg.rec, rb)
    let ok: bool = writer.line(lg.w, s, buf, text)
    if ok { lg.emitted = lg.emitted + 1 }
    return ok
}

// Put a VALID record in the stream saying one was lost, so the gap is visible to whoever
// reads the log rather than only to whoever queries `truncated()`.
//
// It rebuilds from scratch in the same buffer, which is why it can succeed where the real
// record did not — and if even this does not fit, it gives up rather than emitting a
// half-written apology for a half-written record. `truncated()` has already counted it
// either way, so nothing is lost that was not already reported.
fn announce_truncation(mut lg: Logger, mut s: Sink, mut buf: []u8) -> bool {
    var rb: []u8 = rbuf(lg)
    sink.reset(lg.rec)
    lg.jw = json.writer()
    if lg.fmt == LOG_JSON {
        json.begin_object(lg.jw, lg.rec, rb)
        json.key(lg.jw, lg.rec, rb, "level")
        json.put_str(lg.jw, lg.rec, rb, "error")
        json.key(lg.jw, lg.rec, rb, "msg")
        json.put_str(lg.jw, lg.rec, rb, "log record truncated")
        json.end_object(lg.jw, lg.rec, rb)
    } else {
        sink.put_str(lg.rec, rb, "level=error msg=\"log record truncated\"")
    }
    if sink.overflowed(lg.rec) { return false }
    return writer.line(lg.w, s, buf, sink.view(lg.rec, rb))
}

// ---- logfmt values ------------------------------------------------------------

// Does `v` need quoting to survive as one logfmt field?
//
// **Pure, and public, so the platform that ships the log can test the rule that makes it
// parseable** — the same argument `std/syserr`'s tables and `std/syswatch`'s event parser
// make. This predicate is where a logfmt writer is actually wrong: get it too narrow and a
// value with a space silently becomes two fields, or one with an `=` invents a key.
//
// The empty string is quoted too, and that is not an edge case for tidiness: `k=` followed
// by a space is indistinguishable from `k=` at the end of a line, so an unquoted empty value
// is the one case where a reader cannot tell a field is there at all.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn needs_quoting(read v: str) -> bool {
    if v.len == 0 { return true }
    for i in 0..v.len {
        let ch: i32 = v[i] as i32
        if ch <= 32 { return true }          // space and every C0 control, newline included
        if ch == 34 { return true }          // '"'
        if ch == 61 { return true }          // '='
        if ch == 92 { return true }          // '\'
        if ch == 127 { return true }         // DEL
    }
    return false
}

// A logfmt value: bare when it is unambiguous, otherwise a JSON string.
//
// The quoted form is produced by `json.put_str` rather than by a second escaper here, so
// there is exactly ONE escape table in the tree and it is the one `json_test` already
// covers. A fresh `json.Writer` is used so its separator state cannot leak in: with
// `need_comma` false it emits the quoted string and nothing else.
@no_alloc @no_os fn put_logfmt_value(mut s: Sink, mut buf: []u8, read v: str) {
    if needs_quoting(v) == false {
        sink.put_str(s, buf, v)
        return
    }
    var q: json.Writer = json.writer()
    json.put_str(q, s, buf, v)
}
